window.docContents[117].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalE2E.PqxdhInputs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalE2E___PqxdhInputs":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalE2E › PqxdhInputs.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 12 headline theorems depend on it 168 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (13)abbrev Sha512Statestructure PqxdhPrimsstructure PEParamsabbrev initiateTapeLendef initiateKemCoinsdef djbKeydef kyberKeystructure LocalIdentitystructure PreKeyBundleFieldsdef PreKeyBundleFields.bytesdef ofU8def toU8def fitVec\n\n\n\nPQXDH Parameters and Typed InputsTrusted: the statement. Abstract PQXDH cryptographic primitives (PqxdhPrims, PEParams), initiator coin-tape layout (initiateTapeLen, initiateKemCoins), typed local identity and pre-key bundle formats (LocalIdentity, PreKeyBundleFields), and BitVec 8 / UInt8 conversions for SignalPqxdh.Model. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md and Computational/docs/DEVIATIONS.md (DEV-PQ1, DEV-PQ2, DEV-PQ6).\n\n\n\nThe free primitives\n\nabbrev Sha512StateL38–39 ↗#A SHA-512 chaining state: eight 64-bit words.\n\nstructure PqxdhPrimsL41–73 ↗#The PQXDH cryptographic primitives (abstract in the protocol/security model layers, and instantiated to the concrete functions realPqxdhPrims executed by the linked ELF in the closed CLI headlines).DEV-PQ1 (machine model): SHA-512 compression and Kyber/ML-KEM machine code. On the placed CLI ELF (CliBinaryPq.lean), compress512, kyber::{encapsulate, decapsulate}, and mlkem768::incremental::* execute their linked machine code directly, proved against realPqxdhPrims (FIPS 180-4 SHA-512, round-3 Kyber-1024, and FIPS 203 ML-KEM-768 incremental, with SampleNTT divergence excused by DEV-CLI22). Byte strings are fixed-length vectors.DEV-PQ2 (libsignal): the KEM is Kyber1024 round 3, not FIPS 203 ML-KEM-1024. libsignal v0.105.4's kem::KeyType::Kyber1024 calls libcrux_ml_kem::mlkem1024::kyber (the pre-standard round-3 variant, TAG_KYBER_1024 = 0x08). In the closed CLI headlines, encaps / decaps are instantiated to kyber1024EncapsVec / kyber1024DecapsVec (realPqxdhPrims), so the security layer's KEM assumptions must be read as about Kyber1024 round 3. Effect: none here; a disclosure for the security conjunct.decaps is total: Kyber decapsulation never fails (implicit rejection returns a pseudorandom secret), as libcrux's decapsulate returns [u8; 32].\n\nParameters\n\nstructure PEParamsL77–84 ↗#The parameters of the PQXDH statement: the session layer's primitives (C : Crypto, the primitives absE uses) and the PQXDH primitives (Q : PqxdhPrims). Universally quantified in the protocol/security model layers, and instantiated to realPEParams in the closed CLI headlines.\n\nThe coins of init\n\nabbrev initiateTapeLenL88–98 ↗#The coin length of init: 192 bytes.DEV-PQ6 (machine model): the handshake's randomness is a tape of fixed shape. The CLI draws its randomness from the call's coins file, in order (signal_session_coins_fill, cli/src/coins.rs, in-image code). Alice's init makes 34 draws: Alice's base key (one 32-byte fill), the Kyber encapsulation coins (32 next_u32 calls, 4 bytes each, of which initialize_alice_session keeps the low byte, so coin i is byte 4 i of those 128 bytes; initiateKemCoins), and Alice's first ratchet key (one 32-byte fill): 192 bytes. Bob's pre-key recv takes 32 (his first ratchet key). Why: an RNG is a source of fresh bytes, and the game fills the tape. Effect: none; the security layer must draw the Kyber coins as initiateKemCoins of a uniform tape, which is uniform.\n\ndef initiateKemCoinsL100–103 ↗#The 32 Kyber coins init passes to kyber::encapsulate for the tape t: byte 32 + 4 i for i < 32 (DEV-PQ6).\n\nInputs\n\ndef djbKeyL107–108 ↗#A Curve25519 public key as libsignal serializes it: type byte 0x05 and the 32 bytes.\n\ndef kyberKeyL110–112 ↗#A Kyber1024 public key as libsignal serializes it: type byte 0x08 (kem::KeyType::Kyber1024) and the 1568 bytes.\n\nstructure LocalIdentityL114–119 ↗#The caller's identity (me).\n\nstructure PreKeyBundleFieldsL121–141 ↗#A pre-key bundle, typed so that its encoding PreKeyBundleFields.bytes is always well-formed: key type bytes are djbKey / kyberKey's, lengths are fixed, and has_opk is 0 or 1.\n\ndef PreKeyBundleFields.bytesL143–151 ↗#The 1813-byte serialization of a bundle: reg_id has_opk opk_id opk_pub[33] spk_id spk_pub[33] spk_sig[64] kpk_id kpk_pub[1569] kpk_sig[64] id_pub[33], integers LE; with no one-time pre-key, has_opk = 0 and its fields are zero.\n\nBytes as UInt8 (for the link to the PQXDH model)\n\ndef ofU8L155–158 ↗#A byte string of the PQXDH model (Bytes := List UInt8, LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalPqxdh/Model.lean) as machine bytes (as Symbolic/Specifications/Refinement/Concretization.lean: .map UInt8.toBitVec).\n\ndef toU8L160–162 ↗#Machine bytes as a byte string of the PQXDH model. Inverse of ofU8 (ofU8_toU8, toU8_ofU8 in LibsignalBinary/Proofs/E2E/PqxdhSpecTestsPure.lean).\n\ndef fitVecL164–166 ↗#The first n bytes of bs, zero-padded, as a vector.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalE2E/PqxdhInputs.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalE2E.PqxdhInputs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalE2E___PqxdhInputs"}});